      "content": "That's a bit of a middle-ground answer. But looking at the actual outcomes, isn't it basically a fact that prioritizing pathways over enforcement causes the system to break down because of the sheer volume? Wouldn't anyone arguing for heavy enforcement just be responding to that reality? It feels like the \"humanitarian\" side is always assuming the system can handle more, while the enforcement side is the only one looking at the actual capacity limits.",
